+- [20261023-201420-improvement-composing-several-re](20261023-201420-improvement-composing-several-re/EXPERIENCE.md) — **when** The change in this tenacity repo has to work for both the sync and the async variant of an operator or strategy (`retry_base` vs `async_retry_base`). Python's reflected-operator dispatch sends mixed sync/async operands to the async class's `__ror__`/`__rand__`, so a fix that only handles same-family operands passes the pure-sync and pure-async tests but leaves the mixed case behaving differently. **then** In `skills/tenacity-change-contract/SKILL.md`, give each operator the change touches a written rule stating which side of a mixed sync/async expression handles it (`sync_x | async_y` → `async_retry_base.__ror__`) and which direction of absorption is safe: an async container may take sync members, a sync container must never take async ones. Also list the operand mixes the tests must cover (all sync, all async, sync→async, async→sync, plain callable on each side). If the mixed case still behaves differently, it must be reported as unmet, not as a "choice to redirect".
 - [20261023-200259-feature-add-an-enabled-parameter](20261023-200259-feature-add-an-enabled-parameter/EXPERIENCE.md) — **when** A tenacity task adds a run-time option or behaviour switch to `Retrying`/`AsyncRetrying`/`@retry`, where the request is worded around the decorated call but users can also start a run through `for attempt in Retrying(...)`, `async for`, or `TornadoRetrying`. **then** In `skills/tenacity-change-contract` (SKILL.md), name every run entry point explicitly (`Retrying.__call__`, `BaseRetrying.__iter__`, `AsyncRetrying.__call__`, `AsyncRetrying.__aiter__`, `TornadoRetrying.__call__`) and require each to be handled or listed as left out. Saying in general that `BaseRetrying`/`_begin_iter` owns the loop is not enough: agents that had only that sentence still guarded just the `__call__` methods.
 - [20261023-195627-feature-attemptmanager-the-objec](20261023-195627-feature-attemptmanager-the-objec/EXPERIENCE.md) — **when** A tenacity feature task that adds API or a new way to use existing API (a new protocol on an existing object, a new keyword, async/trio variant) where the user docs show the existing usage form without naming the class, so a grep for the class or method name over doc/ finds nothing and the change ships code+test+reno but no docs. **then** In the on-demand skill `skills/tenacity-change-contract`, list user docs as a required touch for features: edit `doc/source/index.rst` (README.rst symlinks to it) next to the existing example. Locate the section by grepping the existing usage snippet (e.g. `with attempt:`), not the class name. Write the example as a `.. testcode::` block that defines but doesn't run async functions. Name it in the skill description so it loads for the change. Keep it out of always-on system.md.
 - [20261023-190929-bug-the-delay-since-first-attemp](20261023-190929-bug-the-delay-since-first-attemp/EXPERIENCE.md) — **when** A tenacity bug fix whose regression test has to observe state during an attempt (a `statistics` key, a `retry_state` field, a callback argument). The "first fail on unfixed code" run can be masked: an exception raised inside the decorated function is retried, and one raised in a callback is hidden by `suppress(Exception)`. So the red run either passes or fails for some other reason. **then** Put the exception-path facts in `skills/tenacity-change-contract`, the on-demand skill that loads for this kind of change: - which test class owns the area (e.g. `TestStatistics`) - read mid-attempt state from a `before`/`after` callback via `retry_state.retry_object` - callback exceptions propagate raw, while exceptions in the wrapped function get retried - only exhausted retries raise `RetryError`, so suppress only `RetryError` - which loop methods are shared through `BaseRetrying.begin()` and which are re-implemented in `tenacity/asyncio/` Don't rely on red-before-green alone to catch a test that fails for the wrong reason.
